WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.730
Sometimes, an actor becomes so intrinsically
associated with a movie character that it's

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:08.280
very difficult to imagine anyone else occupying
that screen space.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.620
In the case of these castings, the actors
were so right for their roles that it seems

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:16.420
completely wrong that they weren't the first
ones the filmmakers had in mind.

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:20.160
Here are some actors who almost didn't get
their very memorable parts.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:21.680
Gene Wilder

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:26.380
While he's best remembered for his iconic
performance as Willy Wonka, Gene Wilder also

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:31.359
collaborated with director Mel Brooks on a
series of classic comedies, such as The Producers,

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.600
Young Frankenstein, and most famously, Blazing
Saddles.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:40.460
This 1974 satire was a savage takedown of
both the Western genre and race relations

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:45.000
in America, and it featured Wilder as the
Waco Kid, a drunken gunfighter who decides

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.620
to help Sheriff Bart face down racist townsfolk
and an evil gang of cowboys, bikers, and German

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:51.120
soldiers.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:56.180
But while Wilder now seems like such an obvious
guy to play the part, Brooks originally wanted

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:59.779
an older actor, somebody who "could look like
an over-the-hill alcoholic."

00:00:59.779 --> 00:01:04.180
He considered going after Dan Dailey — an
actor best known for starring in old-timey

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:07.760
musicals — and thought about casting talk
show host Johnny Carson.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:10.100
Brooks even asked John Wayne to read the script.

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:14.050
And while the Duke thought it was hysterical,
he said he couldn't play in a movie "that

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:18.141
had such low-down dirty talk" — even though
he did promise to be the "the first one in

00:01:18.141 --> 00:01:19.170
line to see it."

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:19.680
"I won't?

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:21.920
The hell I won't."

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.780
Eventually, Brooks hired Gig Young, but he
had to drop out, ironically, because of his

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:28.000
problems with alcohol.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:32.229
Desperate, Brooks finally decided to cast
Wilder — who'd been asking to play the part

00:01:32.229 --> 00:01:35.040
for a while, and the rest is cinematic history.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:36.600
Ed Harris

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:42.380
In 1998, Jim Carrey gave one of his best performances
to date in The Truman Show, but he wasn't

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:46.940
the only one bringing his A-game to Peter
Weir's brilliant take on TV, celebrity, and

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:47.440
religion.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:52.100
Ed Harris also created a memorable role in
Christof, the all-powerful director who sits

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:55.160
in his moon base and controls every detail
of Truman's life.

00:01:55.469 --> 00:01:59.060
But while Harris was perfect in the role,
he actually didn't get the part until two

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:03.060
months after shooting started — and that's
because Christof was actually meant to be

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:04.960
played by a different Hollywood legend.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:10.240
Best known for his performances in movies
like Easy Rider, Apocalypse Now, Blue Velvet,

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:14.720
and Hoosiers, Dennis Hopper was the man originally
cast as Christof, but he didn't last very

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:15.220
long.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:19.480
Speaking with writer Alex Godfrey, Hopper
said he "was on the set for like, two days,

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:23.790
and got fired … Scott Rudin, the producer,
had made an agreement with the director [Peter

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:27.569
Weir] that… if he didn't like what I did
after the first day's dailies then he would

00:02:27.569 --> 00:02:28.220
fire me.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:29.360
And they fired me."

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:33.959
Although it was an insult to Hopper, it also
put Ed Harris in a tough spot, as he started

00:02:33.959 --> 00:02:36.769
shooting just days after signing onto the
project.

00:02:36.769 --> 00:02:41.080
Harris later revealed, "I didn't have much
time to prepare, but it worked out all right.

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:45.340
In fact, it was almost a blessing in disguise,
because I was forced to trust my instincts."

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:47.500
"Good afternoon, good evening, and good night."

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:49.160
Clint Eastwood

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:54.680
Directed by Sergio Leone, A Fistful of Dollars
is the movie that kickstarted Clint Eastwood's

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:59.240
career as the whispering, no-nonsense, squinty-eyed
action hero that we've all come to know.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:04.040
But back in the early '60s, Eastwood was just
a lowly TV actor and nowhere close to the

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:06.340
top of the list when it came to casting choices.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:10.560
In fact, Leone offered the role of the Man
with No Name to pretty much every actor in

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.730
Hollywood before giving the job to Eastwood.

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:18.000
He went after guys you've probably never heard
of, like Rory Calhoun, Henry Silva, and Eric

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:18.500
Fleming.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:23.579
He also tried to lure in bigger names like
Henry Fonda, Charles Bronson, and James Coburn,

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:26.680
but they all shot down his proposal, mainly
over money.

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:31.700
Leone could only pay $15,000, which wasn't
much for an established star.

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:36.230
Fortunately, the Italian director saw an episode
of the TV show Rawhide which featured a young

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:40.849
Eastwood as Rowdy Yates, a naïve, immature
cowboy who's a whole lot friendlier than the

00:03:40.849 --> 00:03:41.820
Man with No Name.

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:46.760
Despite the differences, Leone thought Eastwood
was, quote, "good at getting on a horse."

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:51.319
Soon enough, Eastwood was one of the biggest
movie stars in history, all thanks to a corny

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:53.800
TV show and a bunch of overpaid actors.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:55.320
Casey Affleck

00:03:55.880 --> 00:04:00.800
In the critically-acclaimed film Manchester
by the Sea, Casey Affleck plays Lee Chandler,

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:05.620
a man struggling with grief and guilt in the
aftermath of a terrible tragedy, and he totally

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:06.520
owns the part.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:11.040
In fact, he won an Academy Award for his work
with the role in 2017.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:15.209
But it was actually long-time Affleck family
friend Matt Damon who was initially supposed

00:04:15.209 --> 00:04:16.240
to star in the role.

00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:21.140
The film's plot was originally dreamed up
by Damon and John Krasinski of The Office,

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:24.180
and they asked filmmaker Kenneth Lonergan
to write the script.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:28.720
Damon intended to direct and star, but it
took Lonergan three years to write the first

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:29.220
draft.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:32.120
When he was done, Damon didn't have time to
direct.

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:36.320
He still wanted to play the main character,
but when other projects got in the way, he

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:37.680
passed the role of Lee to Affleck.

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:39.600
"Yeah, he passes on all the good ones."

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:43.631
Damon told The Hollywood Reporter, "This is
one of the best roles I've ever seen for a

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:44.240
leading actor.

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:48.200
I didn't want to hand it off to somebody unless
that was somebody I grew up with and who would

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:49.460
make me happy I gave it up."

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:52.250
All that work and all he got was one lousy
shoutout.

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:56.500
"Thank you also to Matt Damon for creating
this opportunity."

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:58.780
Jodie Foster and Kristen Stewart

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:03.260
Sandwiched in between Fight Club and Zodiac,
Panic Room is a film that's often forgotten

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:05.020
when discussing David Fincher films.

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:10.720
And really, that's too bad, as the 2002 film
is a solid thriller featuring strong performances

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:14.020
from its two female leads, Jodie Foster and
Kristen Stewart.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:19.180
The two play a mom and daughter taking shelter
inside, well, a panic room after a trio of

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:20.700
thieves breaks into their home.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:25.520
But while both actresses do a great job, neither
were the original picks for this gritty suspense

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:26.020
film.

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:29.880
Fincher has revealed that the first actress
chosen for the part of the daughter, Sarah,

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:34.210
was, quote, "very feminine and girlish and
all those things, and just irritated [him]

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:34.800
beyond belief."

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:39.420
Fincher hasn't mentioned the actress by name,
but Hayden Panettiere was reportedly booked

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:42.740
to play Sarah, but dropped out shortly before
filming started.

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:47.090
Fincher then went with Stewart after seeing
both an audition tape and a commercial featuring

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:50.740
the young girl, saying he liked her "tomboy-ish,
androgynous quality."

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:55.100
As for the role of Meg, the mom, the part
originally went to Nicole Kidman, and she

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:58.950
was actually working on the movie for about
two weeks before injuring her knee.

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:02.550
Evidently, the pain was so bad that she was
forced to pull out of Panic Room.

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:06.590
Fortunately for Fincher, Jodie Foster saved
the day, taking the lead role and getting

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:07.700
production moving again.

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:12.260
Interestingly, casting Foster changed the
director's entire vision for the film.

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:16.720
Talking to The Guardian, Fincher explained
that Kidman was all about "glamour and physicality,"

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:20.900
like Grace Kelly in a Hitchcock film, while
with Foster, "it's more about what happens

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:21.900
in her eyes.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:22.540
It's more political."

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:23.980
Vin Diesel

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:28.720
The Fast and the Furious franchise is full
of memorable characters, but Dominic Toretto

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:30.640
is the centerpiece of the series.

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:34.640
This street-racing criminal who puts family
above all else has appeared in every entry

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:39.090
in the series except one, and he's basically
the chassis of the whole Fast and Furious

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:39.590
universe.

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:44.340
Nowadays, it's virtually impossible to think
of anyone besides Vin Diesel playing the part

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:47.050
of Dom because he is the face of the franchise.

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:50.780
But as you've probably guessed by now, Diesel
wasn't number one on the list of actors to

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:51.820
try out for Toretto.

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:56.400
Producer Neal H. Moritz has since revealed
that while actor Paul Walker was Brian from

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:00.610
the get-go, studio executives were keen to
cast Timothy Olyphant as Dom.

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:03.340
However, for unknown reasons, he passed on
the part.

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:08.080
Seeing Olyphant as Dom would've been interesting,
but picking Diesel was obviously the right

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:08.580
choice.

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:14.460
The first Fast and the Furious earned $207
million on a $38 million budget, and the series

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:19.090
has become one of the top-ten highest-grossing
franchises of all-time, largely thanks to

00:07:19.090 --> 00:07:20.260
Diesel's star power.

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:22.260
"The street always wins."

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:26.880
So yeah, it looks like the studio executives
at Universal eventually chose the right man

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:27.440
for the job.

00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:29.500
Emma Stone and Ryan Gosling

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:34.510
Sure, La La Land didn't actually win the Oscar
for Best Picture, but it did win the hearts

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:38.740
of audiences around the world, largely thanks
to the winning duo of Emma Stone and Ryan

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:39.300
Gosling.

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:43.160
But if things had played out a little bit
differently, an entirely different Emma would've

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:44.940
been dancing across the silver screen.

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:49.380
The same goes for Gosling, who got the call
to play jazz pianist Sebastian when writer-director

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:52.810
Damien Chazelle decided to change his original
acting line-up.

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:56.720
Originally, La La Land would have starred
Emma Watson and Miles Teller.

00:07:56.900 --> 00:08:02.000
Back in early 2015, Damien Chazelle said the
only thing he knew for sure about La La Land

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:05.000
was that it would be a musical, and that Teller
would play the male lead.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:08.990
The two had previously worked together on
his critically acclaimed film Whiplash, so

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:10.160
the reunion made sense.

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:14.220
But a whole lot can happen when you're working
on a movie, especially one you started writing

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:15.300
back in 2010.

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:19.920
Along the way, La La Land began to evolve,
and Chazelle eventually decided that Teller

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:21.870
wasn't right for the part after all.

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:26.980
Unfortunately, the young actor didn't take
the news very well, texting the director, "what the f---, bro?"

00:08:27.270 --> 00:08:31.080
Emma Watson reportedly handled things a lot
better, and she was okay with passing on the

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:31.840
part of Mia.

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:36.120
After all, she was already committed to Disney's
live-action reboot of Beauty and the Beast,

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:39.649
and as she explained to Entertainment Weekly,
"I knew I had horse training.

00:08:39.649 --> 00:08:44.069
I knew I had dancing, I knew I had three months
of singing ahead of me, and I knew I had to

00:08:44.069 --> 00:08:45.460
be in London to really do that….

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:48.140
So scheduling conflict-wise, it just didn't
work out."

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:53.060
Of course, Watson's Beauty and the Beast went
on to break the billion dollar barrier, becoming

00:08:53.069 --> 00:08:57.209
one of the highest grossing films ever made,
and Stone would win an Oscar for her La La

00:08:57.209 --> 00:08:58.500
Land performance as Mia.

00:08:58.820 --> 00:09:02.100
So yeah, it looks like things worked out pretty
well for both of the Emmas.

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:06.520
As for Teller, well, he's probably still nursing
a case of whiplash over the director's diss

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:07.480
and dismissal.

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:09.200
James McAvoy

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:14.560
When M. Night Shyamalan's Split hit theaters
in early 2017, audiences were terrified by

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:19.520
James McAvoy's performance as Kevin… and
Patricia… and Hedwig… and Dennis.

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:24.940
Playing a man with 23 personalities — and
a 24th on the way — McAvoy stole the show

00:09:24.940 --> 00:09:26.880
every time he morphed into a new persona.

00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:31.600
But as it turns out, Joaquin Phoenix, who'd
worked with the director on Signs, was actually

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:35.709
Shyamalan's first choice to play Kevin and
each of his individual identities.

00:09:35.709 --> 00:09:39.810
However, Phoenix ended up being tied to another
movie and had to drop out.

00:09:39.810 --> 00:09:44.629
Making things even more complicated, Shyamalan's
second choice, McAvoy, was also committed

00:09:44.629 --> 00:09:45.540
to another film.

00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:50.100
Fortunately for everyone involved with Split,
the Scottish actor's schedule soon cleared

00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:51.540
up, and he was able to sign on.

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:55.779
But stepping into the film so suddenly posed
a bit of a challenge for McAvoy, since he

00:09:55.779 --> 00:09:57.769
had to play nine different characters.

00:09:57.769 --> 00:10:01.639
As as a result of the casting confusion, he
only had "about a quarter of the time" that

00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:05.040
he usually took to prepare for just one character,
let alone nine.

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:10.280
Still, McAvoy was able to pull the roles off
perfectly, scaring audiences and critics alike.

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:11.540
Thanks for watching!

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:14.520
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:16.840
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

